\subsection{Definitions and mathematical statement}
\paragraph{Statement recorded in the supplied source.}
For every complete smooth metric g on $R^{3}$ with $Ric_g\geq 0$ and each integer $k\geq 0$, must the space of real harmonic functions of growth at most degree k have dimension $\leq (k+1)^{2}$, the Euclidean value? Each harmonic function may have its own growth constant.

\subsection{Short English statement}
For every complete smooth metric g on $R^{3}$ with $Ric_g\geq 0$ and each integer $k\geq 0$, must the space of real harmonic functions of growth at most degree k have dimension $\leq (k+1)^{2}$, the Euclidean value? Each harmonic function may have its own growth constant.

\subsection{Sources}
\begin{itemize}
\item[S1] OpenAI, manuscript citation page. Source of the formulation motivating this local problem.
\url{https://github.com/openai/math/blob/adc7f1241b42e322a6451854ab7e4b4c146bf78a/preprints/A-Three-Dimensional-Counterexample-to-Integer-Degree-Harmonic-Dimension-Comparison-September-26-2026/README.md}.
\end{itemize}

