\section*{Harmonic dimension comparison in dimension three}

\subsection{OpenAI's claim}
\textit{Harmonic growth comparison in dimension three}: The companion claims nonnegatively Ricci-curved metrics on $\mathbb R^3$ with more degree-$k$ harmonic growth modes than the Euclidean count by a fixed factor. The metric may depend on the large integer $k$, and prescribed asymptotic volume ratios and small global bi-Lipschitz distortion are available.

\subsection{Outline of the argument}
\textit{Harmonic growth comparison in dimension three}: A finite angular program is chosen before the radial tail and starting scale. Spectral rates are averaged by successively crossing cone geometries, then matched through the radial harmonic system. The transfer is controlled for that fixed program, producing enough independent global modes with the required pointwise integer growth; no uniform estimate in the growing degree is assumed.

\subsection{Scope relative to this problem}
\textit{Harmonic growth comparison in dimension three}: The comparison is restricted to dimension three but still assumes only nonnegative Ricci curvature. A fixed-factor excess at a chosen large integer degree violates the claimed Euclidean count, even though the constructed metric may depend on that degree.

The claimed counterexample lies within the question’s hypotheses and contradicts its universal conclusion.

\subsection{Formal verification}
Family 361 describes Lean coverage for an integer-growth dimension obstruction in dimension sixteen at a fixed degree; the three-dimensional construction is separate.

\textit{Harmonic growth comparison in dimension three}: No manuscript-specific Comparator is listed in the pinned inventory.

The documented selection does not cover the entire manuscript assertion relevant here.

This site has not run the Lean development. These links describe OpenAI’s selected formal statements and should be read with their stated scope.

\subsection{Status caveat}
OpenAI reports that an internal model produced the manuscripts. Their arguments have not been independently verified as proofs by this site. Any portion outside the documented formal scope may contain mistakes, and the catalogue label reports the announced mathematical claim.

